WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
now it's been five years since russia  entered the war in syria in support of  president bashar al-assad  tipping the balance in favor of the  regime assad forces assisted by russia  and iran now control  most of the country kurdish forces still  hold the northeast but have signed a  deal with assad  idlib in the northwest is the last  remaining province under rebel control  with turkey's support asia a ceasefire  for idlib came into  effect in march of this year but  government and russian air strikes are  increasing again  and many in idlib fear a decisive battle  for the province  could start soon dw's burgetta shulker  reports  even when he's making tea plane spotter  abu al-zain's focus is on the job  not to miss a single military plane  taking off from the bases  controlled by the assad regime  the 41 year old has set up a mini  listening station to follow  syrian and russian radio communications  at a secret location  for security reasons along with other  volunteers he monitors all air traffic  over heathcliff  around the  we clock listen in to the pilot's radio  communications  and find out his destination so we know  where the bombs might fall  i do this work for civilians if we can  warn them we can save lives  as soon as he spots anything about zane  warns local civilians  hospitals and first responders using  radio and other networks  he says the number of air raids on etlip  has risen significantly in recent weeks  although the ceasefire agreement is  still in effect  my biggest fear is that the russian air  force will bomb idlib city  because it is so densely populated  in recent weeks the bombs sometimes more  than 20 a day  have mainly targeted less populated  areas  but there's growing concern that assad  and his forces are set to launch  in new offensive also targeting  hospitals and schools  in a similar way to the beginning of the  year when attacks flattened the small  town  of kaffernuran  many families have since returned home  but khaleda  khaled from the local council has no  idea how they'll survive the coming  winter  water supplies hospitals electricity  generators have all been destroyed  and coveted 19 infections are on the  rise  we can't quarantine people to protect  them from the coronavirus  they've got to work to feed their  families  we would need support from relief  agencies in our town  but no one has shown up  hundreds of thousands of people have  sought refuge in the sprawling camps of  northern italy province  here two conditions are appalling but  there's at least  some support from relief organizations  farmer ahmed suleiman is among those who  desperately need that support  a year ago his house was destroyed in an  air raid  one of his sons was killed ahmed lost  both legs  he's grateful for the tent they were  given but he's anxious about the winter  that lies ahead  i'm disabled i can't walk at all  we've got no income nothing at all  ahmed says his wife left him because she  could no longer bear  the gloom and the misery in the tent  since then  his sister has been looking after him  and the three children  they've never known anything but war  never been to school  the children are in very bad shape they  don't listen to me  especially the eldest is driving us  crazy  they just want their mother back  so what does the future hold how can  they possibly keep going  what will they do for heating when  winter sets in what about the  coronavirus  and where can they go next if assad's  forced to start a new offensive  too many questions and no answers  let's bring in daniel gerlach for more  on this he's a middle east analyst and  editor-in-chief of zenith magazine which  focuses on the region  welcome to dw let's start with the  humanitarian  situation how many people are there in  rebel-held areas of idlib  and what are conditions like for them  well the conditions have been  illustrated quite  drastically in the report and in the  reports of the relief organizations  it's very difficult to give accurate  numbers at the moment  about rebel controlled areas circuit  controlled areas  because the front line has been shifting  and  there's also different like areas that  are  under control of different rebel  organizations of course  but we have to see that there is about  maybe 2 million people that are exposed  to military operations to the threat of  an increase of bombings and of course  this is a  very difficult situation that libyans  have been living with for quite some  time  we've heard the the fears in the the  report that  um the syrian government is looking  likely to  uh try and retake um idlib um  are they actually in a position to be  able to do that  i doubt that to be honest um i think it  is absolutely possible  that we will see an increase of military  operations aerial operations  artillery shelling we've seen exchanges  in the last couple of days  um also rebels  targeting regime held areas and regime  troops  but it would be quite untypical for the  syrian regime and the russians to try  a large ground operation now that winter  is approaching  also in light of the pandemic that is  savagely  savagely going after syrians all across  the country of course  syrians are exposed to the coronavirus  we don't have official numbers and  the regime seems to be playing that down  but it's a very very serious threat in  syria at the moment so i doubt that  there would be a major ground operation  but i think there is another rather  cynical and geopolitical aspect to it  um if russia should decide to increase  its pressure to start  a bombing campaign over italy this would  not be so much to reestablish syrian  regime control over idlib  but to put pressure on turkey which is  now  uh of course very busy with the military  escalation the caucasus where turkey is  supporting  azerbaijan and russia supporting armenia  on the other side  and we should not forget that there's  also other conflict scenarios in libya  and the eastern mediterranean  and quite typically russia has been  using this card increasing military  pressure in syria and italy  whenever whenever it was suitable to  force turkey into some sort of a  compromise  and i think this is not so much about  reestablishing regime control over idlib  though of course this would be  president's assad's ultimate goal  and he would try to attempt this before  next year's presidential election  but uh i think what the russians have in  mind here is something very different  okay so it it obviously it's it's quite  a complicated  situation but it does sound just looking  at the russian  involvement in this conflict that russia  is  moving to its own agenda and it has its  own goals that it is looking to  achieve in the area not necessarily um  to  to buttress the syrian regime of course  yeah which could be in line with the  agenda of the syrian regime the  uh russian foreign minister lavrov has  traveled recently with a pretty high  ranking delegation to damascus  to give us i think some reinsurance  reassurance but also to try  uh to like develop some sort of a  political  vision for the future of syria because  the russians have been now  present in syria with uh their military  firepower for five years but in fact  they haven't really  uh they have saved the syrian regime and  they have  gained leverage in the middle east but  they haven't really found a political  solution which i think  they are still interested in presenting  i think the russians  want to keep of course military control  and there's absolutely no question that  they want to  force rebel-held areas into submission  but on the other hand they see that  syria needs some sort of a solution  an authoritarian solution of course at  russian gusto but  it there needs to be political progress  in light of the upcoming presidential  election and so  um the russians are engaged and tried to  involve the united nations they  it was their idea to bring together some  sort of a constitutional committee  with syrians from regime held areas and  from the opposition  to create some compromise and and and  find some sort of a  perspective and they haven't been very  successful with this so far  so i think russia will remain involved  militarily and politically  but sometimes for very different goals  good talking to you it's very clear as  ever daniel gerlach from  zenith magazine thank you  you

